          %h1#algebras_for_modal_logics Algebras for modal logics
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#idea"} Idea
              %li
                %a{:href => "#boolean_algebras_with_operators_baos"} Boolean algebras with operators (BAOs)
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#boolean_algebras"} Boolean algebras
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#operators"} Operators
              %li
                %a{:href => "#examples"} Examples
              %li
                %a{:href => "#varieties_of_modal_and_polymodal_algebras"} Varieties of modal and polymodal algebras
              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_concepts"} Related concepts
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            Classical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositional+calculus"} propositional calculus
            has an algebraic
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model"} model
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Boolean+algebra"} Boolean algebra
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kripke+semantics"} Kripke semantics
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+operators"} modal operators
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/frame+%28modal+logic%29"} frames
            have no relations, so are just sets. If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi W
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
            is such a set, (of worlds), a valuation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                  %mo :
                  %mi Prop
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mn 2
                    %mi W
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V: Prop \to 2^W
            just assigns to each
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi Prop
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p \in Prop
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi w
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi W
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} w\in W
            a truth value,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊤
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \top
            or
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⊥
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bot
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mn 2
                    %mi W
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2^W
            and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semantics"} semantics
            extends the assignment given by
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            to a map of algebras, from the term algebra based on the basic propositional language to the Boolean algebra of subsets of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi W
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            bit-by-bit on the terms. This gives an algebraic interpretation or representation of the terms of the logic in terms of the algebra of subsets of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi W
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+logic"} Modal logics
            also have an algebraic semantics based on a Boolean algebra, but with additional operators that model the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+operators"} modal operators
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+models+for+modal+logics"} geometric semantics
            using
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/frames+%28modal+logic%29"} frames
          %h2#boolean_algebras_with_operators_baos Boolean algebras with operators (BAOs)
          %h3#boolean_algebras Boolean algebras
          %p
            We will, here, consider a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Boolean+algebra"} Boolean algebra
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔹
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{B }
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔹
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi B
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                  %mo ,
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mo ,
                  %mover
                    %mrow
                    %mo ¯
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{B} = (B, +, \cdot, \overline{},0,1)
          %p
            so, for example, for a set
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/power+set"} power set
            Boolean algebra will be
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℙ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝒫
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mo ∪
                  %mo ,
                  %mo ∩
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ∅
                  %mo ,
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{P}(S) = (\mathcal{P}(S), \cup, \cap,-,\emptyset, S),
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} -A
            is shorthand for the complement,
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                    %mo −
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S- A
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
          %h3#operators Operators
          %p
            The
            %strong operators
            that we need to add into the Boolean algebras do not always preserve all the structure:
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition Definition
            %p
              A
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function"} function
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi m
                    %mo :
                    %mi B
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m : B\to B
              is called an
              %strong operator
              on the Boolean algebra,
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝔹
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{B}
              %em additive
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi m
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo +
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi m
                    %mi x
                    %mo +
                    %mi m
                    %mi y
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m(x+y) = m x + m y.
            %p
              The operator,
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi m
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m
              %strong normal
              if
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi m
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m(0)=0
          %p
            Any operator,
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi m
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi l
                  %mo :
                  %mi B
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} l : B\to B
            given by
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi l
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi m
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo −
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} l(x) = (m(x^-))^-.
          %p
            As
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi m
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m
            is additive,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi l
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} l
            is
            %strong multiplicative
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi l
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi l
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mi l
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} l(x\cdot y) = l(x)\cdot l(y),
          %p
            and has
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi l
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} l(1) = 1
            if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi m
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m
            is normal.
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              One of the myriad notations used for the generic modal operators
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ◊
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lozenge
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo □
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi L
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
              is ‘possibility, and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
              is ‘necessity“, and these gave the names to the operators above.
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              A
              %strong Boolean algebra with operators,
              or BAO, of type
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              consists of a Boolean algebra
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝔹
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{B}
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi m
                        %mi i
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m_i
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi …
                    %mo ,
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i = 1,\ldots, n
              of operators on
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
          %p
            BAOs are sometimes called
            = succeed "," do
              %strong modal algebras
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n = 1
            %strong polymodal algebra
            is then used for the general case.
          %p There is no need in the definition of BAOs to restrict to finitely many operators nor to have all the operators being unary. The general theory is discussed in the Survey by Goldblatt (see the references).
          %h2#examples Examples
          .num_example
            %h6#example Example
            %p
              %strong BAOs from frames.
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔉
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi W
                    %mo ,
                    %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{F} = (W ,R)
              be a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/frame+%28modal+logic%29"} frame
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℙ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi W
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{P}(W)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi m
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m
              by, if
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                      %mo ⊆
                      %mi W
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T\subseteq W
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi m
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi w
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi W
                    %mo :
                    %mo ∃
                    %mi t
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi T
                    %mo ,
                    %mi R
                    %mi w
                    %mi t
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m(T) = \{w \in W : \exists t\in T, R w t \}
            %p
              It perhaps pays to interpret this in the case where
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/preorder"} preorder
              and when it is an equivalence relation. In the first case, this will be the set of states less than or equal to something in
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            .num_lemma
              %h6#lemma Lemma
              %p
                The function
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi m
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m
                is a normal operator.
            %p The proof is a simple manipulation of the definitions.
            %p
              The dual operator
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi l
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} l
              is given by
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi l
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi T
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mi w
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi W
                      %mo{:lspace => "mediummathspace", :rspace => "mediummathspace"} ∣
                      %mo ∀
                      %mi t
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi W
                      %mo ∖
                      %mi T
                      %mo ,
                      %mo ¬
                      %mi R
                      %mi w
                      %mi t
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} l(T) = \{w\in W\mid \forall t\in W \setminus T, \neg R w t\}
            %p
              It is easy to extend this example to
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔉
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi W
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi R
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi …
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi R
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{F} = (W ,R_1,\ldots, R_n)
              with the result being a BAO of type
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          .num_example
            %h6#example_2 Example
            %p
              %strong The Lindenbaum-Tarski algebra of a modal logic.
            %p
              Suppose
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Λ
                    %mo ⊆
                    %msub
                      %mi ℒ
                      %mi ω
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Lambda \subseteq \mathcal{L}_\omega(n)
              is a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/normal+modal+logic"} normal modal logic
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lindenbaum-Tarski+algebra"} Lindenbaum-Tarski algebra
              has a natural BAO structure, for which see the above page.
          %h2#varieties_of_modal_and_polymodal_algebras Varieties of modal and polymodal algebras
          %p The following is a list of some of the main equationally defined classes of (poly)modal algebras. (For convenience each has been given a separate entry.)
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/temporal+algebras"}> temporal algebras
                ;
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closure+algebras"}> closure algebras
                ;
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monadic+algebras"}> monadic algebras
                \.
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+model+for+modal+logic"} geometric model for modal logic
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+type+theory"} modal type theory
          %h2#references References
          %p General books on modal logics that include information on algebraic models include:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Patrick+Blackburn"}> Patrick Blackburn
                , M. de Rijke and Y.
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Venema"} Venema
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Modal Logic
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Marcus+Kracht"}> Marcus Kracht
                ,
                %em Tools and Techniques in Modal Logic,
                Studies in Logic and the Foundation of Mathematics, 142, Elsevier, 1999.
          %p There is an excellent short survey article (versions of which are available on the web):
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Robert+Goldblatt"}> Robert Goldblatt
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Algebraic Polymodal Logic: A Survey
              %p IGPL, 8, (2000) pages 393–450, Special Issue on Algebraic Logic, edited by Istvan Nemeti and Ildiko Sain.
          %p
            Discussion of modal logic in terms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coalgebra"} coalgebra
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+coalgebra+of+an+endofunctor"} terminal coalgebra of an endofunctor
            is in
          %ul
            %li
              Corina Cirstea, Alexander Kurz, Dirk Pattinson, Lutz Schröder and Yde Venema,
              %em Modal logics are coalgebraic
              (
              %a{:href => "http://eprints.soton.ac.uk/267144/1/ModalCoalgRev.pdf"}> pdf
              )
